1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,249
A brand-new bridge once swayed so

2
00:00:02,249 --> 00:00:03,724
hard it tore itself apart.

3
00:00:03,724 --> 00:00:04,248
Why?

4
00:00:04,248 --> 00:00:05,769
This happened in 1940,

5
00:00:05,769 --> 00:00:08,019
only four months after the bridge

6
00:00:08,019 --> 00:00:09,515
opened to traffic.

7
00:00:09,515 --> 00:00:11,428
The wind that morning was only

8
00:00:11,428 --> 00:00:13,198
about 64 kilometers an hour,

9
00:00:13,198 --> 00:00:14,907
barely a strong breeze.

10
00:00:14,907 --> 00:00:16,475
So was this just resonance,

11
00:00:16,475 --> 00:00:18,376
like a singer shattering a glass?

12
00:00:18,376 --> 00:00:19,245
Not quite.

13
00:00:19,245 --> 00:00:21,121
The wind kept pumping energy into

14
00:00:21,121 --> 00:00:23,248
the bridge's own twisting motion until

15
00:00:23,248 --> 00:00:24,847
it grew out of control.

16
00:00:24,847 --> 00:00:26,245
Why did it fail at all?

17
00:00:26,245 --> 00:00:28,048
The deck was solid steel plate,

18
00:00:28,048 --> 00:00:29,518
built thin to save money,

19
00:00:29,518 --> 00:00:30,966
so wind pushed it like a

20
00:00:30,966 --> 00:00:31,551
sail.

21
00:00:31,551 --> 00:00:33,691
Engineers rebuilt it with open trusses

22
00:00:33,691 --> 00:00:35,036
and wind-tunnel testing,

23
00:00:35,036 --> 00:00:36,924
now standard on every big bridge.

24
00:00:36,924 --> 00:00:38,713
Do you think the first builders

25
00:00:38,713 --> 00:00:40,039
could have caught this?
